### TL;DR  
`map.setFog()` is a **Mapbox-only** API.  
In MapLibre GL JS you get “haze” (the gradual fade to the sky) from the **sky object**, not from a fog layer.  

```js
// 1️⃣  Add a raster-dem source (the same one you already use for hillshade/terrain)
map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
  type: 'raster-dem',
  url: 'https://my.tileserver.com/terrain/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
  tileSize: 256,
  // encoding must match your DEM (e.g. "terrarium" or "mapbox")
  encoding: 'terrarium'
});

// 2️⃣  Turn the DEM into 3-D terrain (required for fog to work)
map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1});

// 3️⃣  Tilt the camera so the horizon is visible (pitch > 0)
map.easeTo({pitch: 60, bearing: 0});   // any pitch > 0 will do

// 4️⃣  Add a sky object – this is where the “haze” lives
map.setSky({
  // colour of the sky itself
  'sky-color': '#88ccee',          // light blue
  // colour of the horizon line (where sky meets ground)
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',      // white
  // colour of the fog that blends the terrain into the sky
  'fog-color': '#c0e0ff',          // a slightly desaturated blue
  // how quickly the fog fades from the horizon toward the sky
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,        // 0 = hard line, 1 = very soft
  // how much the fog blends into the horizon line
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  // how much the fog blends into the ground (terrain)
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

That’s it – the map now has the same “haze at the horizon” effect you used to get with `map.setFog()`.

---

## Why `setFog` no longer works

| Mapbox GL JS (v2) | MapLibre GL JS (v2-v3) |
|-------------------|------------------------|
| `map.setFog({color, ...})` | **No such method** – the call is ignored and you see a blank background above the terrain. |
| Fog is a *root-level* object, not a layer. | Fog is part of the **sky** object (`map.setSky()` or a `sky` entry in the style). |
| Works even without terrain (just a colour gradient). | Fog only renders **when 3-D terrain is active** (`map.setTerrain()`). Without terrain the sky is just a solid colour. |

So after the migration you have two things missing:

1. **A sky object** (instead of a fog object).  
2. **Enabled terrain** (otherwise the fog never draws).

---

## Full Minimal Example (HTML)

```html
<!DOCTYPE html>
<html>
<head>
  <meta charset="utf-8" />
  <title>MapLibre – horizon haze (sky + fog)</title>
  <script src="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.js"></script>
  <link href="https://unpkg.com/maplibre-gl@4.4.0/dist/maplibre-gl.css" rel="stylesheet" />
  <style>
    body, html { margin:0; padding:0; height:100%; }
    #map { width:100%; height:100%; }
  </style>
</head>
<body>
<div id="map"></div>
<script>
  const map = new maplibregl.Map({
    container: 'map',
    style: {
      version: 8,
      sources: {},
      layers: []   // we’ll add everything programmatically
    },
    center: [-122.447303, 37.753574],
    zoom: 12,
    pitch: 0,      // start flat, we’ll tilt later
    bearing: 0
  });

  map.on('load', () => {
    // 1️⃣ DEM source
    map.addSource('terrain-dem', {
      type: 'raster-dem',
      url: 'https://demtiles.example.com/{z}/{x}/{y}.png',
      tileSize: 256,
      encoding: 'terrarium'   // or "mapbox"
    });

    // 2️⃣ Enable terrain (required for fog)
    map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem', exaggeration: 1});

    // 3️⃣ Tilt the view so we can see the horizon
    map.easeTo({pitch: 60, duration: 2000});

    // 4️⃣ Add sky (this is where the haze lives)
    map.setSky({
      'sky-color': '#88ccee',
      'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
      'fog-color': '#c0e0ff',
      'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
      'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
      'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
    });

    // (Optional) add a hillshade or color-relief layer so you can see the terrain
    map.addLayer({
      id: 'hillshade',
      type: 'hillshade',
      source: 'terrain-dem',
      paint: {
        'hillshade-exaggeration': 0.4,
        'hillshade-illumination-anchor': 'map',
        'hillshade-shadow-color': 'rgba(30,40,80,0.45)',
        'hillshade-highlight-color': 'rgba(255,240,200,0.4)'
      }
    });
  });
</script>
</body>
</html>
```

Open the page, wait a couple of seconds for the terrain tiles to load, and you’ll see a soft blue-white haze that fades into the sky – exactly what `map.setFog()` used to give you.

---

## Quick Checklist after a migration

| ✅ What to verify | How to check |
|-------------------|--------------|
| **Terrain source** (`raster-dem`) is added and `map.setTerrain()` is called. | `map.getTerrain()` returns an object, not `null`. |
| **Camera pitch** > 0. | `map.getPitch()` returns a number > 0. |
| **Sky object** is present. | `map.getSky()` returns an object with `sky-color`, `fog-color`, etc. |
| **Fog colours** are set (otherwise you’ll just see a plain sky). | In the sky object, `fog-color` is a non-transparent colour string. |
| **No `map.setFog` calls** left in the code (they do nothing). | Search for `setFog(` – remove or replace with `setSky`. |

If any of the above is missing, the horizon will appear as a solid background colour (often the default `#000000` or the `background` layer colour) and you’ll think the “haze” disappeared.

---

## Advanced Tweaks (optional)

| Property | What it does | Typical values |
|----------|--------------|----------------|
| `sky-horizon-blend` | Softness of the line where sky meets horizon. | `0.2` (sharp) → `0.8` (very soft) |
| `horizon-fog-blend` | How quickly the fog colour blends into the horizon line. | `0.3` – `0.7` |
| `fog-ground-blend` | How far the fog reaches down onto the terrain. | `0.2` (thin) → `0.8` (deep) |
| `sky-color` | Base colour of the sky (above the horizon). | Light blues, e.g. `#88ccee` |
| `horizon-color` | Colour of the actual horizon line. | Usually white or a very light tint |
| `fog-color` | Colour of the haze that mixes terrain → sky. | Slightly desaturated version of `sky-color` |

Play with those numbers until the haze looks natural for your map’s colour palette.

---

## Common Pitfalls

| Symptom | Cause | Fix |
|---------|-------|-----|
| **No haze, just a solid blue sky** | Terrain not enabled (`map.setTerrain` missing) or `pitch: 0`. | Add `map.setTerrain(...)` and set a non-zero pitch. |
| **Fog never appears, even with terrain** | `fog-color` omitted or set to `transparent`. | Provide a visible colour (e.g. `rgba(200,200,255,0.6)` or `#c0e0ff`). |
| **Fog looks too strong / covers the whole map** | `fog-ground-blend` set to `1` (full blend). | Reduce to `0.3-0.5`. |
| **Console shows “unknown property: fog-color”** | Using an old MapLibre version (< 2.4) that didn’t yet implement the sky object. | Upgrade to the latest MapLibre GL JS (≥ 2.4, preferably the current 4.x). |
| **`map.setSky` throws “is not a function”** | Using a version of MapLibre that predates the sky API (pre-2.4). | Upgrade the library. |

---

## TL;DR Code Snippet (copy-paste)

```js
// Assuming `map` is already created and the DEM source exists:
map.setTerrain({source: 'terrain-dem'});   // terrain required for fog
map.easeTo({pitch: 60});                  // see the horizon
map.setSky({
  'sky-color': '#88ccee',
  'horizon-color': '#ffffff',
  'fog-color': '#c0e0ff',
  'sky-horizon-blend': 0.5,
  'horizon-fog-blend': 0.5,
  'fog-ground-blend': 0.5
});
```

That restores the horizon haze you had with `map.setFog()` in Mapbox GL JS, using the MapLibre-compatible **sky + fog** API. Happy mapping!
